WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:05.660
There's a reason that the old, retro-futuristic
dreams of monorail travel didn't come true.

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:09.030
Nowadays, monorails are mostly known as attractions
in theme parks,

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:11.059
or short systems around airports,

00:00:11.059 --> 00:00:13.889
or as impractical, expensive boondoggles.

00:00:13.889 --> 00:00:17.560
Sydney, Australia, actually demolished theirs
a few years ago.

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:20.250
Sure, monorails looked cool, but objectively,

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:21.680
they're a pretty terrible idea.

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:25.580
But in some places in the world: they do work.

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:27.590
This is Wuppertal, in Germany,

00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:29.090
where they don't just have a monorail:

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:33.090
they have a century-old suspended monorail
that still carries

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:37.460
about 80,000 people a day along miles and
miles and miles of track.

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:40.050
Never mind your 1980s dreams of World's Fairs:

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:42.820
construction on this started in the 19th century,

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:45.399
and it's been kept up to date ever since.

00:00:45.399 --> 00:00:47.530
It's like something out of 'Thunderbirds'.

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:49.649
There's a fantastic story about it once carrying
an elephant --

00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:53.829
which panicked, fell out into the river, and
-- amazingly -- survived.

00:00:53.829 --> 00:00:57.130
So why are monorails such a terrible idea?

00:00:57.130 --> 00:00:58.670
Well, compared to other options,

00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:01.809
they generally fail on cost and reliability.

00:01:01.809 --> 00:01:04.809
First, you need a constantly elevated track
--

00:01:04.809 --> 00:01:08.030
the term is 'grade-separated' -- along the
whole route.

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:10.500
Even in the rare case it's down at ground
level,

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.180
you've got to put huge concrete blocks for
it to run on

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:17.470
rather than just some rails. And given all
that cost,

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:20.280
governments generally just build one track

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.810
so if something breaks down, the whole system
fails,

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:25.920
and passengers have to be evacuated from a
great height.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:28.970
You can solve exactly the same problems as
a monorail

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:32.080
with light rail that doesn't always need an
expensive elevated track.

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:35.510
Or a tram, that can run along existing streets

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:37.610
with just some rails laid down.

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:43.979
Or you could just use the simplest option:
a bus.

00:01:43.979 --> 00:01:44.720
Doesn't look as cool, though.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.080
